WEBVTT

1
00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:42.430
Toni Wahrstätter: GM…

2
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.000
lightclient: GM.

3
00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.160
marc | wolovim: Hey everyone, we'll get started in just a couple minutes.

4
00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:37.539
marc | wolovim: All right, it's alright if we got a little smaller group here to start. A lot of things going on, other meetings going on at the same time, including the CLPQ Breakout.

5
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.889
marc | wolovim: So let's just kick this thing off.

6
00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:51.489
marc | wolovim: Welcome, everyone. This is, the Frame's Transaction Breakout Call Number 8. Today is November 17th, 2026.

7
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.019
marc | wolovim: Let's get into it.

8
00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:58.089
marc | wolovim: There's been a world of difference between

9
00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:01.109
marc | wolovim: Where we're at in today and the last time

10
00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.009
marc | wolovim: Breakout Number 7 call happened.

11
00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:14.260
marc | wolovim: So I think the first and best thing we can do is to get caught up on… on the latest, everything that's… all the discussions that have happened, workshops and so on, since

12
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:20.009
marc | wolovim: DEVCON and some, R&D L1.

13
00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:27.590
marc | wolovim: days just ahead of DevCon, so… Matt, do you want to get us started from there?

14
00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:31.990
lightclient: Sure, thanks, Mark.

15
00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:41.359
lightclient: Yeah, DevCon Mumbai was a bit over a week ago, and we did have a bunch of events.

16
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:46.760
lightclient: In terms of the Frames discussions, mostly was…

17
00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:54.629
lightclient: you know, happening during this, Frames Breakout session that I held during the Thursday of DEF CON.

18
00:05:54.820 --> 00:06:01.409
lightclient: And we talked about… a bunch of topics, but I can share with you a few of the main…

19
00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.540
lightclient: outcomes that I think are important to note from that breakout.

20
00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:13.869
lightclient: The first one, to me, is this, like, EIP8288 proposal.

21
00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:23.969
lightclient: which you've probably seen, like, Vitalik and other people tweeting about it, is kind of the idea of how, in the future, we're going to be able to aggregate

22
00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.759
lightclient: these, like, these, like, large PQ signatures into a single block.

23
00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:32.250
lightclient: proof for all of the PQ signatures in the block.

24
00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:37.670
lightclient: That idea, like, extends a bit beyond signatures, where maybe we can also…

25
00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:42.440
lightclient: Verify, you know, aggregate a bunch of different proofs across

26
00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:48.420
lightclient: you know, all of the user transactions. Maybe that's them redeeming from a privacy pool, maybe it's…

27
00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:54.969
lightclient: You know, any kind of zero-knowledge proof that a user might want in their dependency list.

28
00:06:55.100 --> 00:07:04.839
lightclient: So that was kind of the original idea with 8288, and we talked about how frames can be compatible with it. 8288 basically introduces a new frame mode currently.

29
00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:23.579
lightclient: And it's a bit at odds with how we're approaching, you know, signatures or proofs today with frames. Like, we have this object, this signature list, which exists outside of the, you know, list of frames in the transaction. And we did that for a lot of reasons.

30
00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:43.310
lightclient: Which, yeah, we can get into in the call if you want, but, basically, now that it seems that 8288 is, you know, we're moving it up in terms of priorities, we're wanting to become, you know, fully PQ faster, to me, it makes a bit of sense to try to…

31
00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:50.280
lightclient: come to a solution that's forward compatible with that. And I think the signature list…

32
00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:57.919
lightclient: Yeah, basically, we can either extend the signature list to support something like 8288.

33
00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:02.760
lightclient: There's some downsides in terms of, well, the protocol has to…

34
00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:07.240
lightclient: Basically, add support for every proof type.

35
00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:11.749
lightclient: And maybe there's, like, a generic proof type that we can support, in the signature list.

36
00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:20.920
lightclient: Or we can kind of go down this path, which is basically how 8288 proposes in EIP, is that we add a new frame mode, and then that frame mode becomes the…

37
00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:35.290
lightclient: signal that this is something that the protocol can potentially aggregate. And how this actually works is still a question. I think there's been a handful of different proposals, and now the latest idea is this, like, lean PCS idea.

38
00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:43.809
lightclient: Which I haven't fully groked, to be honest, and so… I need to spend a little bit of time with that and figure out what the right way to…

39
00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:50.050
lightclient: Implement this in the Frame's envelope so that we basically support this frame mode.

40
00:08:50.610 --> 00:09:15.600
lightclient: So that it works today with, you know, ACDSA signatures, with, you know, whatever signature format we're gonna support in frames, and then in the future with SPHINCS and ZK proofs and all that stuff. So that… yeah, that's kind of like a big takeaway that I had from the, the, you know, from Mumbai and from that breakout, is that we need to figure out how to make frames forward compatible with that. So that likely means removing the signature list.

41
00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:19.320
lightclient: That's kind of, like, where my thinking is going right now.

42
00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:32.870
lightclient: And what exactly that looks like. I tried to sketch out an idea of it in the breakout, but that was before the LeanPCS kind of became the… the main idea, and so I'm not sure if what I had proposed

43
00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.610
lightclient: fits with LeanPCS. Basically, I was thinking that we could have a proof mode type.

44
00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:48.229
lightclient: And you would… you know, it would look a lot like the signature list, except that it would live inside the frames. And the signature type would be defined by the address.

45
00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:58.099
lightclient: Instead of by the, you know, type… the signature type byte in the signature list. So yeah, these are some things that we need to figure out pretty soon. The other…

46
00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:06.879
lightclient: there's probably two more important things. So then we also talked a bit about the interaction of 7906 in frames. There was a point that…

47
00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:20.719
lightclient: With 7906, if you're making a transaction assertion for a paymastered Frame transaction, you have this unfortunate situation where typically, because of the public mempool rules, you need to pay the ERC20 token

48
00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:29.509
lightclient: to your Paymaster, if that's the type of, like, sponsored transaction you're doing. You need to do that after the verify phase ends, but assert will revert

49
00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:33.340
lightclient: All of the, you know, execution frames.

50
00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.829
lightclient: Back to the point where the transaction was approved.

51
00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:45.650
lightclient: And so, you don't really, you know, you want to be able to use a cert to protect your transaction, but you don't want it to potentially revert Paymaster

52
00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:58.950
lightclient: operation, the Paymaster payment. And so we kind of looked at a few possibilities about how to get around this. We looked at maybe adding a new flag that basically lets you flag that a frame is not allowed to be reverted by a cert.

53
00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:11.410
lightclient: a handful of other possibilities. I didn't like any of them very much, because I think that the way that 7906 is written today is really nice, because you have, like, high certainty, and there's, like, no tricks to it.

54
00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:28.100
lightclient: You know, you basically submit your transaction, and you kind of know that it's going to be safe, and it's only going to do what you, you know, expect that you're asserting. And so I think adding these extra flags and stuff adds some complexity that I'm afraid will make it not as,

55
00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:40.720
lightclient: not as easy for users to use. So we're still thinking a little bit about what the right way to handle this is. I think, like, it is possible that maybe 7906 just doesn't work with Paymaster transactions in the public mempool to start.

56
00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:59.890
lightclient: And there's, like, a whole class of things that would be nice if we could allow some state changes in the, like, verification phase of the transaction. And so we also talked about that a little bit, like, these storage rules for the mempool, basically trying to understand

57
00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:01.469
lightclient: you know.

58
00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:20.080
lightclient: understand, like, what a type of… what is the storage that the user is accessing? Does that storage derive from the sender's address? And if it does, then you have, like, pretty good, abilities to reason that this storage is kind of owned by the user, even though it may not be in the user's account. And so I think, like, some combination of that is…

59
00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:26.839
lightclient: yeah, a likely path to maybe allowing these, like, ERC20 token transfers before the transaction gets approved.

60
00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:28.830
lightclient: In the verification phase.

61
00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:40.769
lightclient: So that's the second thing. And then the third major topic that we looked into was the… the hashing of the transaction. Etan basically proposed that…

62
00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:46.309
lightclient: Yeah, I mean, if we really want this to be the last transaction type, then it would be good to…

63
00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:53.410
lightclient: You know, applied a lot of this, like, thinking that we've had on the transaction representation.

64
00:12:53.540 --> 00:13:11.180
lightclient: For, like, a long time to the frame transaction. And, I mean, basically that… that essentially just means, like, using SSZ for hashing and serialization. And then we get the nice properties of the hashtree root for the Frame Transaction. You know, we have the stable containers now, so…

65
00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:17.820
lightclient: When you make a proof about a Frame transaction, you know that index that you're proving against is going to be stable across forks.

66
00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:22.899
lightclient: So… Yeah, I mean, overall, I think I'm, like, obviously…

67
00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:28.120
lightclient: understand that it's better… that SSZ is a better…

68
00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:33.600
lightclient: way to represent the transaction. The reasons that we haven't done it in the past, you know.

69
00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:40.269
lightclient: a lot of it is that there's a lot of inertia of RLP on the EL, and so…

70
00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:52.730
lightclient: adding the SSZ library has been something that people didn't want to do. There was arguments that we don't have a good SSZ library for whatever language. I think these arguments are starting to die down a bit in my mind, like, we have…

71
00:13:53.390 --> 00:13:55.650
lightclient: Yeah, a lot of ability to generate

72
00:13:56.130 --> 00:14:03.150
lightclient: pretty good code, especially on, you know, very clear problems such as SSZ, so I think that that is not the biggest issue.

73
00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:23.289
lightclient: I'd be curious, like, what people think, if they're supportive of looking at doing SSZ with this transaction type, if people think, you know, it's not a good idea because we're opening a huge can of worms. I don't know. I think that, like, in isolation of timelines and of, like, the complexity, SSZ transactions are better.

74
00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:41.989
lightclient: In a lot of ways, and so we should probably look at doing it. And I guess that's kind of, like, the, you know, summary of this breakout. And the way… the thing we took away from it is that we've got about a month before people kind of take off for the, you know, end-of-year holidays, and we really want to get the frame spec

75
00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:47.939
lightclient: you know, maybe not spec… you know, maybe not, like, frozen for Hegota, but we want to finish…

76
00:14:48.220 --> 00:15:05.160
lightclient: up with these, like, larger changes, so the two larger changes here mostly being, like, moving the signature… removing the signature list, doing something 8288 forward compatible, and then deciding if we want to do the SSZ serialization hashing. And if we want to do that, then we should try to get that finalized in the next month.

77
00:15:05.980 --> 00:15:09.810
lightclient: So yeah, that's kind of the DevCon recap.

78
00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.870
lightclient: Happy to answer any questions.

79
00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:16.100
marc | wolovim: Cool, thank you, Matt.

80
00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:24.599
marc | wolovim: That's, thanks also, you brought it home there in the closing statement. Wanted to frame this as,

81
00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:31.630
marc | wolovim: We're in a little bit of limbo here, as there are conversations, outside of

82
00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:48.599
marc | wolovim: this, about what the eventual scope of Hegota should be, so I think it is wise to focus on, basically the immediate future, what makes sense to keep people unblocked and using their cycles well now, so…

83
00:15:50.350 --> 00:16:06.610
marc | wolovim: to that effect, you covered a lot of ground there, at least 3 big areas, and highlighted those two large changes, some decisions that could be made before the end of the year, being the 82888 forward compatibility stuff, or SOC hashing.

84
00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:11.269
marc | wolovim: So the general question I'll leave before I hand it off to Justin is.

85
00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:15.840
marc | wolovim: What… what is the most important,

86
00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:23.060
marc | wolovim: use of our time here, given that sort of limbo situation. Go ahead, Justin.

87
00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:44.620
Justin Florentine: Yeah, I kind of want to echo, maybe some of the comments that Ivan's making in chat, but also that, you know, I came to this meeting expecting that we were going to be reducing scope and trying to kind of speed things up and focus frames a little bit more on things that were germane to the signatures. So, with regard to that, I think 8288 makes a ton of sense, aggregation's good.

88
00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:57.789
Justin Florentine: whether or not introducing SSZ to get some convenient transaction routing, I think, to me, that's a little bit… that's going in the wrong direction. So that's… that's not something that I find really compelling.

89
00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:18.609
marc | wolovim: Okay, I'm following some conversation in chat. Yvonne, do you want to make your 8288 statements out loud?

90
00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:37.799
Iván | ethrex: Sure. Thanks. Thanks, Mark, and thanks, Matt, for the summary. No, it's mainly what I said in the chat. I like 8288 ideas. I know that we are not doing it in Hegota, because it's really big. I like the idea of splitting it in

91
00:17:37.969 --> 00:17:42.329
Iván | ethrex: more than one EIP. I think the…

92
00:17:42.539 --> 00:17:57.989
Iván | ethrex: I think even though 8288 is bigger, we should do at least one part of it, or the solution that we need to come up with needs to be forward compatible with it. And I think that

93
00:17:58.569 --> 00:18:07.889
Iván | ethrex: I'm about to open a PR with a… to start discussing this, but I think the easiest solution is to add

94
00:18:08.289 --> 00:18:10.039
Iván | ethrex: the dependency frame.

95
00:18:10.359 --> 00:18:25.269
Iván | ethrex: With the links… with only the SPHINCS support. And then, yeah, and in future forks, we can continue iterating on… on this EIP, that… I see the EIP as… as an end goal, more… more than a…

96
00:18:26.059 --> 00:18:34.989
Iván | ethrex: any EIP that you implement and do. That's why I believe splitting it makes sense.

97
00:18:35.539 --> 00:18:38.379
Iván | ethrex: But yes, I think the easiest solution is to take

98
00:18:38.709 --> 00:18:49.389
Iván | ethrex: the existing ideas of 8288, and try to have the minimal thing we could have for frames. I think we still have time.

99
00:18:50.079 --> 00:18:59.109
Iván | ethrex: to work on this and see if it makes sense, and I'll be… I'll be opening APR soon, so we can start discussing.

100
00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:09.070
lightclient: So I guess, like, yeah, the main goal I want to solve with this, like, you know, forward compatibility

101
00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:11.959
lightclient: with 8288, is that I want it…

102
00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:17.099
lightclient: To not require the users to change their wallet again whenever we get aggregation with 8288.

103
00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:19.150
Iván | ethrex: Yeah, exactly. That makes a lot.

104
00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:23.410
lightclient: That's the main… yeah, that's the main thing that we need to try to solve.

105
00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:32.260
Iván | ethrex: Yes, yes. And, well, in the proposal I'll be posting,

106
00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:44.610
Iván | ethrex: what I think is, if we keep the shape of 8288, people won't need to change again.

107
00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:50.269
Iván | ethrex: And they'll be safe of this stuff that you're worried about, that we all worry about.

108
00:19:53.960 --> 00:19:54.690
marc | wolovim: Toni?

109
00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:03.269
Toni Wahrstätter: Yeah, so with 8288 transactions, as far as I understand it, transactions will come with a sidecar.

110
00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:09.310
Toni Wahrstätter: And the cycar would actually contain their signature. So there's also this,

111
00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:29.099
Toni Wahrstätter: the step in between where we could already introduce that type of transactions, which of course increases complexity, but on the path to 8288 with aggregation, we need to do it anyway, and you could just not aggregate yet, because it feels like aggregation is currently

112
00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:36.400
Toni Wahrstätter: The most complex part of this proposal, whereas we could already, take the signature.

113
00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:47.389
Toni Wahrstätter: Away from the transaction object, propagate it separately, and then just place the signatures in the block body, or in some separate objects.

114
00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:51.210
Toni Wahrstätter: Like, this can already be done without aggregation.

115
00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:57.080
Toni Wahrstätter: And I guess we should already… I guess Matt's proposal here is already, like, future compatible.

116
00:20:57.260 --> 00:20:58.510
Toni Wahrstätter: To work with that.

117
00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:11.579
marc | wolovim: Matt, you're pushing back on the idea of sidecars, though, already?

118
00:21:13.190 --> 00:21:18.680
lightclient: Yeah, I mean, I just don't see the value of separating the signature from the transaction right now.

119
00:21:19.070 --> 00:21:21.920
lightclient: Because we're not gonna be making use of it, so…

120
00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:26.849
lightclient: I would probably say just include it in the transaction itself.

121
00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:30.460
lightclient: And it's not going to be hashed, so…

122
00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:36.889
lightclient: It's not required for the client to have it in this way, but we can remove it in the future when we need to.

123
00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:46.109
Toni Wahrstätter: Yeah, this can be done too, of course. When I say Saika, I'm still… what I mean is, it's still, like.

124
00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:53.550
Toni Wahrstätter: in the same object on the network. It's more like, in the actual block, we could already

125
00:21:53.790 --> 00:22:03.879
Toni Wahrstätter: segregated, basically, just like Bitcoin is doing with SegWit. And we will need that anyway for 8288 plus frames.

126
00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:10.120
Toni Wahrstätter: But yeah, I'm also happy with, just keeping the signatures in the proof for now.

127
00:22:19.480 --> 00:22:23.979
marc | wolovim: Bonsai asks, wouldn't the size of the signatures be a problem without aggregation?

128
00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.849
lightclient: Yeah, I mean, I think this is something that we need to…

129
00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:39.100
lightclient: you know, push back on this idea that it's not possible for Ethereum to work without signature aggregation.

130
00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:42.150
lightclient: I think, you know, especially on the EL,

131
00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:48.240
lightclient: Everyone is not gonna switch to post-quantum signatures from day one.

132
00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:55.700
lightclient: And, you know, even if, looking at, like, the transaction load we have today, even if we have, like, several hundred transactions with

133
00:22:56.300 --> 00:23:02.949
lightclient: the post-quantum signature, which is, you know, like, 4 per kilobytes. We're only looking at a few megabytes per block.

134
00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:21.160
lightclient: Which is a lot, but it's definitely in the realm of what's possible for us to propagate. I think on the CL, the problems are a bit more difficult, because you're trying to aggregate, like, tens of thousands of validators per block, whereas, like, for us, we're just dealing with, you know, much smaller numbers.

135
00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:24.639
lightclient: So, aggregation is good, like, we want to get to that point.

136
00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:37.459
lightclient: But I think we shouldn't stop ourselves from shipping post-quantum cryptography into the protocol, because we don't have the aggregation scheme fully figured out. I think we need to think about how…

137
00:23:37.790 --> 00:23:45.210
lightclient: like, how do these teams work? What's, like, a black box aggregation look like? And how can we build something that's forward compatible with that to the best of our ability?

138
00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:54.349
lightclient: And then we need to ship that, because I think this, like, diffusion of this PQ crypto into users' wallets is gonna take a very, very, very long time.

139
00:23:54.790 --> 00:24:00.789
lightclient: And the sooner that we make it, Production-ready for people to use.

140
00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:13.289
lightclient: post-quantum crypto, the better we're gonna, you know, the better percentage of people that are gonna be migrated to that crypto scheme is when the time comes that they need to be,

141
00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:15.240
lightclient: Safe from those threats.

142
00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.299
marc | wolovim: At the risk of…

143
00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:34.599
marc | wolovim: introducing an idea that is not quite on topic. Jobud, do you mind if I put you on the spot? I saw that you made a comment in the agenda recently about doing some signature size measurements.

144
00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:38.770
marc | wolovim: Is that applicable to discuss here?

145
00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:42.379
marc | wolovim: I only skimmed the… The comments so far.

146
00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:56.839
marc | wolovim: I see you unmute, but I don't hear you yet.

